From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: PW_PS4_FORCE_EXEC=0 keeps no-exec on for data pages

alloc_module() turns no-exec off for the whole process when one module
lacks NX_COMPAT (FalloutNV.exe 1.4.0.525 does, as do binkw32, libvorbis,
libvorbisfile, ssce5432 and atimgpud). mprotect_exec() then sends every
readable range to the glue with PROT_EXEC, and on direct memory each such
request below 16 MiB is an exec migration: copy out, unmap, map zero-filled
read/write flexible memory, copy back (pw_wine_dmem_glue_ps4.c:1448-1540).
Between that mmap and the end of the copy-back another thread reads zero
without a fault and its stores are overwritten (the glue's own comment at
:1634-1639 says so), and the migrated range stays in the flexible pool.
1049 counted 57,052 migrations on at least six threads in one Fallout run
(docs/HANDOFF-2026-10-26.md, "The speed question").

With PW_PS4_FORCE_EXEC=0 in the environment (a profile's [debug] env line),
virtual_set_force_exec(TRUE) leaves force_exec_prot off, so only pages
whose Windows protection is executable ask for PROT_EXEC and migrate. Code
that runs from memory it did not make executable then takes an instruction
fetch fault (err 0x15). Unset, or any other value, keeps Wine's behaviour.
One ERR line says the setting took.

New code, LGPL-2.1-or-later. Applies after 1009, 1010, 1024, 1025 and 1027.
---
diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
index b890af1..e4df2d8 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
@@ -5997,6 +5997,37 @@ NTSTATUS virtual_uninterrupted_write_memory( void *addr, const void *buffer, SIZ
 }
 
 
+#ifdef __PS4__
+/* PW_PS4_FORCE_EXEC=0 (a profile's "[debug] env" line) keeps no-exec on.
+ *
+ * Wine turns no-exec off for the whole process as soon as one loaded module
+ * lacks IMAGE_DLLCHARACTERISTICS_NX_COMPAT (alloc_module() in loader.c;
+ * FalloutNV.exe is one), and mprotect_exec() then asks for PROT_EXEC on every
+ * readable page: each heap commit, decommit clear and protection change.
+ * On the PS4 an executable request on direct memory is an exec migration
+ * (protect_exec_range() in pw_wine_dmem_glue_ps4.c): the range is copied out,
+ * unmapped, mapped again as zero-filled read/write flexible memory and copied
+ * back. Until the copy-back passes a byte, another thread reads zero there
+ * without a fault, and a store it makes there is overwritten; the migrated
+ * pages then stay in the flexible pool.
+ *
+ * With PW_PS4_FORCE_EXEC=0 only pages whose Windows protection is executable
+ * ask for PROT_EXEC. A program that runs code from memory it did not make
+ * executable then faults on the instruction fetch (x86 page-fault error 0x15)
+ * instead. Read once; unset or any other value keeps Wine's behaviour. */
+static BOOL ps4_keep_no_exec(void)
+{
+    static int cached;
+
+    if (!cached)
+    {
+        const char *value = getenv( "PW_PS4_FORCE_EXEC" );
+        cached = (value && value[0] == '0' && !value[1]) ? 1 : -1;
+    }
+    return cached > 0;
+}
+#endif
+
 /***********************************************************************
  *           virtual_set_force_exec
  *
@@ -6008,6 +6039,15 @@ void virtual_set_force_exec( BOOL enable )
     sigset_t sigset;
 #ifdef __PS4__
     ULONG_PTR stack_addr = (ULONG_PTR)&sigset;
+
+    if (enable && ps4_keep_no_exec())
+    {
+        static LONG logged;
+
+        if (!__atomic_exchange_n( &logged, 1, __ATOMIC_RELAXED ))
+            ERR( "PW_PS4_FORCE_EXEC=0: no-exec stays on; only executable pages ask for PROT_EXEC\n" );
+        return;
+    }
 #endif
 
     server_enter_uninterrupted_section( &virtual_mutex, &sigset );
